description
Clicking Synonyms retrieves synonyms of the selected word from the dictionary server. 
examples
Select the word "write" and click Synonyms.  The spelling tool prints in its typescript something like this:
warnings
Many words have no synonyms. You are not informed as such by the spelling too.
stop/undo 
Impossible.
contact
John Maxwell
description
Clicking Pronunciation retrieves the pronunication of the selected word from the dictionary server. 
examples
Select the word "casuistry" and click Pronunciation.  The spelling tool prints in its typescript something like this:
warnings
The table defining the standard linguistic equivalents of the  dictionary server pronunication symbols is not yet on-line.  Please be patient.  In the future, this service might be tied into the voice server. 
stop/undo 
Impossible.
contact
John Maxwell
description
Clicking WordNerd retrieves the "wordNerding" of the selected words from the dictionary server.  The wordNerding of a list of words is defined as all those words whose definition contains at least WNMinKeyWords (see following comment) of the selected words. 
Note: If invoked with the right mouse button then WNMinKeyWords will be defaulted to the number of words in the selection.  If the first "word" of the selected words is a valid number then it is used as the WNMinKeyWords value for this wordNerding -- it does not affect WNMinKeyWords for future WordNerding.
Three WordNerd parameters can be set from the command tool.
WNMinKeyWords: displays the current value of WNMinKeyWords.
WNMinKeyWords x: defines the threshhold WNMinKeyWords as x.
WNMaxWords: displays the current value of WNMaxWords.
WNMaxWords x: return no more than x words in result.  Words are returned starting from most intersections down to fewest interesections.
WNRef NerdName: defines the Nerd reference to be used.  The EtymologyNerd is reverse-indexed only on the etymological portion of a definition (the part contained within the brackets). The WordNerd is reverse-indexed on the non-etymological portion of a definition portion.  (NerdName defaults to WordNerd)
The WordNerd also accepts more complex queries than those described above.  More specifically, the "Nerding" of the lists of words (Wa1, Wa2, ...,  Wan) (Wb1, Wb2, ..., Wbn)....(Wz1, Wz2, ...Wzn) is evaluated as the disjunction of the  successive nerdings  "Wa1 Wb1 Wz1" , "Wa1 Wb1 Wz2", ...."Wan Wbn Wzn-1" "Wan Wbn Wzn". Refer to the last example below.
examples
Select the words "army officer" and click WordNerd. (Assume MinKeyWord is set to 1 and MaxWords to 200)  The spelling tool prints in its typescript something like this:
Select the word "voice" and click WordNerd. (Assume WNRef is set to EtymologicalNerd)  The spelling tool prints in its typescript something like this:

description
This button causes the Spelling Tool to pare down its in-memory data structures as much as possible, while still maintaing correct behavior.  One consequence is that it tries to write out any word list insertions not yet written to their files.  Another is that it deletes all the bit tables it has computed from the various collections of word lists it has dealt with.  A consequence of this that you'll see is that the list of word lists available for inserting into will become empty.
examples
You just realized that the last three insertions shouldn't really have been inserted.  So you reset the Spelling Tool, then edit the file you mistakenly inserted into.  When you resume spell-checking, the first thing the Spelling Tool will do is read again the appropriate files to build a bit table, and it will thus see the effects of your edits.
warnings
If garbage collection really works, this should only cost some time --- the time to rebuild the bit tables once you start using them again.  If garbage collection is less that wonderful, you could conceivably lose more VM when the new bit tables are built.
stop/undo 
Impossible.
